WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Copies and references
Passing an int by value creates a copy. Changing the parameter does not change the original variable. int& provides a reference to the original.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Explanation
Changes through a reference are visible outside the function. Understand whether a function reads or modifies its argument.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Trace the code
#include <iostream>
void byValue(int x) { x += 5; }
void byReference(int& x) { x += 5; }
int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 10;
    byValue(a);
    byReference(b);
    std::cout << a << " " << b;
}

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Result
10 15

Only a copy of a changed; the original b became 15.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Remember
This complete example compares both approaches. Track a and b separately. Const references are also useful for reading large objects.
